# ACMP in-app user management — the Keycloak service account's identity and grant (ADR-0038).
#
# ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH, deliberately machine-readable and deliberately NOT prose. Two things read
# this file and they must never drift:
#   - deploy/keycloak/reconcile.sh, which creates-or-updates the client and sets this exact grant
#   - scripts/check-realm-export.mjs, which fails CI if the deployed grant is not exactly this set
#
# A shell-sourceable KEY=value file rather than JSON because the Keycloak image ships bash + kcadm
# and NO jq or curl (see reconcile.sh) — so the reconciler can read this directly, with no new
# runtime dependency (CON-001).

# The confidential client whose service account performs invites, role assignment, forced sign-out
# and guest disable. Confidential + service-accounts-only: no standard flow, no direct grants.
ACMP_KC_ADMIN_CLIENT_ID=acmp-admin-svc

# THE MINIMUM GRANT, PROVEN ON UAT 2026-08-12 — NOT COPIED FROM DOCUMENTATION (OQ-070).
# With NO grant the very first call is refused (POST /users -> 403), so a grant is necessary; with
# exactly {manage-users} all nine calls KeycloakAdminClient.cs makes succeed. A one-element
# sufficient set whose empty subset is refused is minimal by construction.
#
# ⚠ THE SET IS UNCHANGED BY SC-011, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN HOPED. Widening the port with
# a read (ListUsersAsync, the DEC-046 reconciliation) added a ninth call, and against Keycloak 26.0
# with exactly this grant GET /users returns 200 — so the port grew and the credential did not.
# ⚠ The shape that WOULD have needed more is worth knowing: GET /roles/{name}/users is 403 here, so
# the adapter reads role mappings per user. A "fewer calls" refactor toward it needs a wider grant.
#
# ⚠ THE OBVIOUS GUESS WAS WIDER. `manage-users + view-realm` was the candidate, on the theory that
# the role-mapping calls need view-realm for Keycloak's canMapRole check. THEY DO NOT. Adding it
# would hand the service account a read over the entire realm configuration for nothing.
#
# Before adding anything here: re-run scripts/probe-keycloak-grant.mjs and show which call is
# refused without it. A refusal is a signal to read it, not to widen (ADR-0038).
ACMP_KC_ADMIN_ROLES="manage-users"

# Never grantable. realm-admin is the answer this design exists to avoid: it would let the one
# outbound write ACMP has reconfigure the realm that authenticates everybody.
ACMP_KC_ADMIN_FORBIDDEN_ROLES="realm-admin"
